{
  "slug": "typing-class-progress",
  "title": "Typing Class Progress",
  "topic": "dynamic-programming",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "pattern": "dynamic programming",
  "aka": "Longest Increasing Subsequence",
  "order": 10,
  "story": [
    "<p>A night-school typing class records each student's speed, in words per minute, at the end of every session. Speeds go up and down: a tired evening, a hard text, a good night's sleep. At the end of term the teacher writes a progress note that quotes some of the sessions, in date order, where each quoted speed is <strong>strictly faster</strong> than the one quoted before it.</p>",
    "<p>The longer the chain of improving sessions, the more encouraging the note. What is the longest chain she can quote for a student?</p>"
  ],
  "task": [
    "<p>Write <code>longestImprovement(speeds)</code>. Return the length of the longest list of sessions you can pick, keeping their original order, where each picked speed is strictly greater than the previous picked speed. The picked sessions do not need to be next to each other.</p>",
    "<p>An empty record gives 0. Equal speeds do not count as an improvement.</p>"
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "0 ≤ speeds.length ≤ 2,500",
    "0 ≤ speeds[i] ≤ 200"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [[42, 38, 45, 40, 47, 41, 43, 50]],
      "expected": 5,
      "explanation": "Quote 38, 40, 41, 43, 50. Starting with the first session, 42, gets you at most 42, 45, 47, 50, which is 4. The best chain starts with a dip."
    },
    {
      "args": [[30, 30, 30]],
      "expected": 1,
      "explanation": "Every session is the same speed. A chain needs each speed to be strictly faster, so the best is one session on its own."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Take the session with speed 43 in the first example. Suppose the chain must <em>end</em> at 43. Which earlier sessions could come just before it?",
    "Any earlier session with a smaller speed: 42, 38, 40 or 41. The best chain ending at 43 is one longer than the best chain ending at any of those. So it helps to know, for every session, the best chain that ends exactly there.",
    "Let <code>best[i]</code> be the length of the longest improving chain that ends at session <code>i</code>. Every session alone is a chain of 1. Then <code>best[i] = 1 + max(best[j])</code> over earlier <code>j</code> with <code>speeds[j] &lt; speeds[i]</code>.",
    "Fill <code>best</code> from left to right with a double loop: for each <code>i</code>, scan every <code>j &lt; i</code>. The answer is the largest value anywhere in <code>best</code>, not just the last one, because the chain can end at any session."
  ],
  "bruteForce": [
    "<p>Try every subset of sessions, check whether it is strictly increasing, and keep the longest. With 2,500 sessions there are 2²⁵⁰⁰ subsets. Even 40 sessions means about a trillion.</p>",
    "<p>Two quick shortcuts are wrong. The longest run of <em>consecutive</em> rising sessions misses chains that skip bad days. Greedily taking every session that beats the last one you took fails on <code>[1, 9, 2, 3, 4]</code>: it takes 1, 9 and gets stuck.</p>"
  ],
  "signature": {
    "function": "longestImprovement",
    "params": [
      {
        "name": "speeds",
        "type": "int[]"
      }
    ],
    "returns": "int"
  },
  "starter": {
    "js": [
      "/**",
      " * @param {number[]} speeds  words per minute, one per session, in date order",
      " * @return {number} length of the longest strictly improving chain",
      " */",
      "function longestImprovement(speeds) {",
      "  // Your code here",
      "}"
    ],
    "py": [
      "def longest_improvement(speeds: list[int]) -> int:",
      "    # Your code here",
      "    pass"
    ]
  },
