  "compare": "exact",
  "solution": {
    "js": [
      "function longestImprovement(speeds) {",
      "  const n = speeds.length;",
      "  const best = new Array(n).fill(1); // best[i] = longest chain ending at session i",
      "  let answer = 0;",
      "  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {",
      "    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {",
      "      // session j can come right before session i if it was slower",
      "      if (speeds[j] < speeds[i] && best[j] + 1 > best[i]) best[i] = best[j] + 1;",
      "    }",
      "    answer = Math.max(answer, best[i]);",
      "  }",
      "  return answer;",
      "}"
    ],
    "py": [
      "def longest_improvement(speeds):",
      "    n = len(speeds)",
      "    best = [1] * n  # best[i] = longest chain ending at session i",
      "    for i in range(n):",
      "        for j in range(i):",
      "            # session j can come right before session i if it was slower",
      "            if speeds[j] < speeds[i] and best[j] + 1 > best[i]:",
      "                best[i] = best[j] + 1",
      "    return max(best, default=0)"
    ],
    "walkthrough": [
      "<p>A chain has a last session. Fix it, and the question gets small: the best chain ending at <code>i</code> is session <code>i</code> added to the best chain ending at some earlier, slower session. Compute that for every <code>i</code> from left to right.</p>",
      "<p>Trace <code>[42, 38, 45, 40, 47, 41, 43, 50]</code>:</p>",
      "<ol>",
      "<li>42: nothing before it. best = 1.</li>",
      "<li>38: 42 is not slower. best = 1.</li>",
      "<li>45: 42 and 38 are slower, both with 1. best = 2.</li>",
      "<li>40: only 38 is slower. best = 2.</li>",
      "<li>47: the slower ones have at most 2 (45 or 40). best = 3.</li>",
      "<li>41: slower are 38 (1) and 40 (2). best = 3.</li>",
      "<li>43: slower are 42, 38, 40, 41; the best of them is 41 with 3. best = 4.</li>",
      "<li>50: everything before is slower; the best is 43 with 4. best = 5.</li>",
      "</ol>",
      "<p>The answer is the largest value, 5. With 2,500 sessions the double loop does about 3 million comparisons, which is quick. A cleverer method with binary search gets O(n log n); it is covered in <a href=\"/algorithms/longest-increasing-subsequence.html\">this lesson</a>.</p>"
    ],
    "time": "O(n²)",
    "timeWhy": "For each session, scan every earlier session once.",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "spaceWhy": "One chain length per session.",
    "takeaway": "When a subsequence must keep order and obey a rule between neighbours, define the state as \"best answer that ends exactly at i\". Each state looks back at all compatible earlier states, and the final answer is the best over all end points."
  },
